# Q13 — Web app accepts ?auth_token=base64("opencode:<password>")?
Date: 2026-10-13. Container agency-spike-q. Token computed locally from the
throwaway password (value never logged).

## Commands + trimmed output

$ curl -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' /api/info
401                                            # no credential rejected
$ curl -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' "/api/info?auth_token=<base64(opencode:PW)>"
200                                            # POSITIVE: query token accepted
$ curl -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' "/api/info?auth_token=<base64(nope:invalid)>"
401                                            # NEGATIVE: bad token rejected
$ curl / | head -c 120
<!doctype html>...<title>OpenCod...            # / serves the app shell (200, public)

Source: review/opencode/packages/server/src/middleware/authorization.ts:12,32-39
  AUTH_TOKEN_QUERY = "auth_token"; query value decoded as Basic credentials.

## Observation
Yes. First navigation with ?auth_token=<base64("opencode:<password>")>
authenticates API calls (200 on /api/info); absent/bad tokens get 401.
The / document itself is public; auth gates /api/*. No manual-sign-in
fallback needed.

## Verdict: yes
